## The pipeline (this context's slice)

```yaml
engineering_pipeline:
  0_sync:      component-engineer regenerates coded tokens from the published Figma DS; diff
  1_components: build/extend {{COMPONENT_LIB}} components + Storybook stories; token-bound (component-engineer)
  1b_parity:   PARITY GATE — Storybook screenshot vs the Figma source; zero unbound, zero drift
  2_publish:   version + publish {{COMPONENT_LIB}} so prototype AND app consume the same version
  3_app:       compose the shared library into the validated screens; wire nav/storage/native (app-engineer)
  4_device:    DEVICE GATE — verify on real hardware what emulators can't prove (auth, push, rollover, signed builds)
  5_build:     build the signed release; verify by artifact (build-uploader)
  6_upload:    eas submit → intended track; CONFIRM by re-reading the track (build-uploader)
  7_record:    record version + commit + track + what shipped
  feedback:
    ds_gap:      "{{COMPONENT_LIB}} needs a token/component the Figma DS lacks" → {{DS_REPO}}, before coding
    lib_gap:     "a screen needs a component the library lacks" → component-engineer, before app build
    flow_back:   "the flow doesn't work once coded" → {{STUDIO_REPO}} (product-designer/ui-designer)
    ds_republish: "Figma DS republished" → component-engineer re-syncs tokens + re-checks parity
    build_fail:  build/upload fails or a device check fails → back to app-engineer, never forward
```

---

## The gates this context adds (beyond the design + usability gates)

1. **Parity gate** (`component-engineer`) — the coded component matches its Figma source and is fully
   token-bound (zero drift, zero literals), verified by a Storybook screenshot, not by eye.
2. **Device gate** (`app-engineer`) — the things only real hardware proves (auth on a store-signed
   build, push, background/time behavior) are verified on a device, not inferred from an emulator.
3. **Release gate** (`build-uploader`) — verify-by-artifact + honest declarations + upload confirmed by
   re-reading the store track. A green command is not a shipped release.

These compose with the studio's design-review + usability gates — the same "prove it, don't assume it"
discipline, carried into code and release.

